Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Sofrito Burns The Signal Relay
- Story & Context: In July 1976, Ismael patched a hot skillet’s thermal sensor into the District Four traffic controller during a neighborhood block party, causing every light on Calle Ocho to blink in time with Xiomara’s opening marimba solo. The sudden gridlock trapped three police cruisers, turning an attempted noise bust into an all-night underpass carnival powered by spliced powerlines. The heat was so intense the grease from the street-cooks' cauldrons literally dripped into the open amplifier racks below. "Pass the bitter lard because this grid belongs to the dancers tonight," shouted Hector over the roaring tape-saturating chorus.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Grainy 1970s film photo of a sizzling iron skillet sitting atop a rusting electrical transformer box, bright red and green traffic lights blurring in the background with glowing marimba bars vibrating in high contrast.

Track 2: ¿Quién Invitó A Chago Al Sancocho?
- Story & Context: Written after infamous street mechanic Chago "El Brujo" accidentally dropped his wrench into the master marimba box during a live broadcast on illegal pirate radio, shorting out the studio monitors but generating a subterranean hum that became the track's iconic sub-bassline. Ismael kept the recorder running while Chago tried to argue his way out of paying for the burnt copper coils. The track captures the paranoid tension of late-night studio lockdown during the 1977 curfews. "If you drop iron in my broth, brother, you better bring a bigger spoon," screams Hector over a ferocious, glitching 108 BPM marimba montuno.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric vintage aesthetic image of a dimly lit 1970s recording control room, microbrew coffee cups and half-eaten stew pots sitting on a massive analog mixing desk dripping with exposed copper wires.

Track 3: The Blender Swallowed Her Gold Tooth
- Story & Context: Long after the original El Hueco venue was demolished by city bulldozers in 1979, this track remained as a ghost-story anthem among San Juan’s night-shift highway crews. It recounts the mythic final night of singer Luisa "La Vaca" who lost her prized gold molar into an industrial citrus juicer during a chaotic backstage victory meal. The rhythmic clatter of metal against blade was recorded on a portable cassette deck and looped into the track's haunting, echo-drenched outro marimba solo. "Keep chewing the cement, my child, until the highway forgets our names," whispers a distant vocal track through a telephone filter as the beat slowly disintegrates into tape hiss.
